{
  "pedal": "Boss DD-8 Digital Delay — stereo: what each output carries, mode by mode (OUTPUT A and OUTPUT B)",
  "comment": "The DD-8 with both outputs connected, never measured before: a first look, so test signals first and guitar after. Per mode (Standard, Analog, Tape, Warm, Mod): clicks 3 s apart (each repeat's time, level and polarity on each side, read directly), two 0.1 s noise bursts at -12 dB 3 s apart (each repeat's tone on each side; under the input clip), then the focused reamp's stabs (the same on guitar). Standard's clicks also at Time 9:00 and 3:00 (how the second side's delay follows the Time knob: a fixed offset, a ratio, the same) and one click at Feedback 3:00 with a long tail (which side each repeat of a long train lands on: straight or crossing over). A steady 1 kHz sine in Standard and Mod (the two sides' level and phase while the repeats pile up; Mod's sweep on each side), Standard's stabs also at Feedback 3:00 with a long tail (a pattern across many repeats), Mod also on the strummed chords. No sweep: with the repeats on, a sweep lands on top of its own echoes; the bursts give the tone per repeat instead. A plug in OUTPUT B may change what OUTPUT A carries: compare with the mono session of the same day (2026-11-09-focused), same settings. The pedal's stereo output type as it is set: note it in the session if known. The CARRYOVER switch as in the mono session (on). Clock positions: 7:00 = fully counter-clockwise, 5:00 = fully clockwise. One knob per step but for Feedback going back to 12:00 with the mode.",
